Jury Selection
Before trial begins, potential jurors are called to the courthouse for jury service, and the set of potential jurors for a given case forms a panel known as the jury pool or venire.

Counsel for the parties have the opportunity to examine potential jurors (either directly or through questions given to the judge, who then questions the prospective jurors), and can then ask the judge to dismiss a member of the pool for cause if the questioning reveals a justification for excusing or disqualifying someone from service in the case. For example, after the basic contours of the dispute are described to the venire, someone who personally knows one of the parties, or who has already made up their mind about the outcome, will be dismissed for cause. This process of examining the jury is known as voir dire.

Depending on the rules of a given court, each party will also be entitled to a certain number of peremptory challenges. These allow the party to dismiss a member of the jury pool without cause.

INTRODUCTORY NOTE TO RULE 47 AND 28 U.S.C. § 1870
Rule 47 and 28 U.S.C. § 1870 set forth conditions terms under which juries may be selected, including the use of peremptory challenges.

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 47
Selecting Jurors

(a) Examining Jurors. The court may permit the parties or their attorneys to examine prospective jurors or may itself do so. If the court examines the jurors, it must permit the parties or their attorneys to make any further inquiry it considers proper, or must itself ask any of their additional questions it considers proper.

(b) Peremptory Challenges. The court must allow the number of peremptory challenges provided by 28 U.S.C. § 1870.

(c) Excusing a Juror. During trial or deliberation, the court may excuse a juror for good cause.

