28 U.S.C. § 1870
Challenges

In civil cases, each party shall be entitled to three peremptory challenges. Several defendants or several plaintiffs may be considered as a single party for the purposes of making challenges, or the court may allow additional peremptory challenges and permit them to be exercised separately or jointly.

All challenges for cause or favor, whether to the array or panel or to individual jurors, shall be determined by the court.



•The court may examine prospective jurors directly or allow parties to do so, and the court may excuse jurors for good cause

•In civil cases in federal court, each party gets three peremptory challenges

NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON RULE 47 AND 28 U.S.C. § 1870
Traditionally, peremptory challenges allowed counsel to have a member of the jury pool dismissed for any reason or no reason at all, and counsel would have no obligation to articulate any reason for striking that person. The notion here is that trial lawyers should be able to rely on their instincts to strike persons who their gut tells them will be biased against their clients. In principle, at least, peremptory strikes protect the value the legal system places on an impartial jury. But does “any reason at all” really mean any reason? And are there other values at stake? Our next case, Edmonson v. Leesville Concrete, considers these questions.
